        Figure 1: The molecular mechanism of cyanoacrylate anionic polymerization, initiated by surface moisture nucleophiles and propagating through resonance-stabilized carbanions.
      </p>

      <!-- SECTION 3 -->
      <section class="section" id="section-3">
        <h2>3. The Role of Moisture: Cyanoacrylate Surface Moisture as Initiator</h2>
        <p>
          The activation of the polymerization reaction depends entirely on the presence of <strong>cyanoacrylate surface moisture</strong> acting as the initiating nucleophile. In a standard ambient environment, all solid substrates naturally adsorb a microscopic, invisible layer of water molecules on their surfaces. When the liquid monomer is squeezed into a thin film between two substrates, these adsorbed water molecules donate hydroxyl ions (OH-) to the electron-deficient carbon double bonds, initiating the instant bonding process.
        </p>
        <p>
          This moisture-initiated reaction highlights why the joint thickness is highly critical. If the adhesive layer is too thick, the surface moisture cannot diffuse effectively throughout the bulk monomer volume, resulting in an incomplete cure or a severely weakened bond core. Consequently, cyanoacrylates perform exceptionally well when applied as extremely thin films (typically 0.05 to 0.1 mm), where the surface-area-to-volume ratio is maximized, ensuring rapid and complete curing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Furthermore, the chemical nature of the substrate surface significantly influences this initiation phase. Alkaline or basic surfaces (such as metals and certain plastics) contain an abundance of nucleophiles, accelerating the curing process. In contrast, acidic substrates (such as wood, leather, or paper) donate protons that neutralize the hydroxyl ions, retarding or completely inhibiting polymerization. For these challenging materials, formulators develop specialized surface-insensitive grades or recommend basic accelerators to facilitate proper curing.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 4 -->
      <section class="section" id="section-4">
        <h2>4. Curing Dynamics: Liquid to Polymer and the Stabilizer Package</h2>
        <p>
          An <strong>instant adhesive formulation</strong> must balance two opposing requirements: it must remain completely stable as a liquid inside the bottle for months, yet polymerize within seconds when applied to a substrate. To achieve this, chemical formulators incorporate a highly balanced stabilizer package. This package consists of acidic stabilizers, which scavenge any trace anions or basic molecules that might enter the bottle, and radical stabilizers, which prevent spontaneous radical-induced curing.
        </p>
        <p>
          The primary anionic stabilizer used is typically a volatile acid gas, such as sulfur dioxide (SO2) or organic sulfonic acids. These acidic molecules maintain a highly acidic environment inside the container, keeping the monomers protonated and preventing initiation. When the adhesive is dispensed as a thin film, the volatile acid gas evaporates, and the overwhelming concentration of surface moisture neutralizes the remaining non-volatile acid stabilizers, allowing polymerization to proceed unhindered.
        </p>
        
        <div class="box" style="margin: 30px 0; background: var(--navy-dark); color: #ffffff; border-left: 5px solid var(--gold); padding: 24px; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0; font-family: 'Source Serif 4', serif;">
          <strong style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif; color: var(--gold); font-size: 1.15rem; display: block; margin-bottom: 8px;">Key Insight</strong>
          <span style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6;">
            Maintaining the delicate balance of acidic stabilizers in the bottle is critical. An excess of sulfur dioxide will severely retard the cure speed on the substrate, while a deficiency will lead to premature gelling (polymerization) inside the bottle, highlighting the need for strict quality control.
          </span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section class="section" id="section-5">
        <h2>5. Industrial Applications and Performance Comparisons</h2>
        <p>
          Cyanoacrylates are widely used across diverse industries, from high-precision electronics assembly and medical device fabrication to automotive trim bonding. However, selecting the correct adhesive system requires a solid understanding of how cyanoacrylates compare to other structural adhesives like epoxies, polyurethanes, and structural acrylics. For instance, while cyanoacrylates excel in cure speed and thin-film bond strength, they are historically brittle and exhibit lower temperature resistance than epoxies.
        </p>
        <p>
          To assist design engineers in making informed selections, the following comparison matrix details the physical and chemical performance profiles of the four primary industrial adhesive classes. This comparison helps clarify which system is best suited for specific environmental, structural, and throughput conditions:
        </p>

        <!-- TABLE WRAPPER -->
        <div class="table-container" style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 30px 0;">
