                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Very High (Elastic)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
                <td style="padding: 15px; font-weight: 600; border: 1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Acrylic (Structural)</strong></td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Fast (5-30 mins)</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">High (18-28 MPa)</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Medium-High (135-150&deg;C)</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Medium-High</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          As shown in the table, cyanoacrylates provide unparalleled curing speeds, making them ideal for high-throughput assembly line processes. In applications requiring bonding complex geometries or rubber substrates, the low viscosity of standard formulations ensures excellent wetting and joint coverage. For comprehensive engineering guidance on choosing adhesive classes, you can consult our detailed guide on <a href="/adhesives-and-sealants-technology" target="_top">adhesives and sealants formulations and technology</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- INFOGRAPHIC IMAGE -->
      <div class="image-slot" style="margin-bottom: 30px; border-radius: var(--radius-lg); overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid var(--border);">
        <img
          src="https://cdn.globalaerosols.com/images/cyanoacrylate-adhesives-fast-cure-mechanism/cyanoacrylate-adhesives-fast-cure-mechanism-infographic.webp"
